        "content": "{\n  \"ratings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1553\",\n      \"applicability_evidence\": \"The proposal directly implements automatic event logging for high-risk systems as required by Article 12 to meet the logging mandate.\",\n      \"applicability\": 4,\n      \"effectiveness_evidence\": \"Integrating logging into training, validation and deployment ensures logs for lifetime events and traceability as mandated by Article 12(2).\",\n      \"effectiveness\": 4,\n      \"acceptability_evidence\": \"Building instrumentation to comply with EU AI Act logging requirements is a standard, legally required business practice.\",\n      \"acceptability\": 4,\n      \"implementability_evidence\": \"Adding hooks to existing experiment tracking requires engineering effort and pipeline modifications but uses current infrastructure.\",\n      \"implementability\": 3,\n      \"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\": \"The proposal weaves logging into core R&D workflows rather than treating it as an after-the-fact compliance add-on.\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness\": 3,\n      \"originality_evidence\": \"Integrating automated logging into ML pipelines is a common, widely discussed compliance engineering practice.\",\n      \"originality\": 1\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal P1553: Embed Mandatory Logging Into Training Pipeline...\n\nWe need rate six subscales in order: applicability, effectiveness, acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources...\n\nProposal embeds automated event-logging modules directly into training, validation, deployment workflows so captures start-end timestamps... conforming to Article 12(2) categories before release.\n\nDoes it solve identified problem directly related? Yes do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. It applies directly to meet logging mandate. So applicability = 4.\n\nEvidence sentence: The proposal directly implements automatic event logging for high-risk systems as required by Article 12.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nReasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? If you could do it, it would solve main problem. Logging integrated from day one captures timestamps, input references... conforming to Article 12(2). Seems solves logging mandate. Probably 4.\n\nEvidence: Integrating logging into training/validation/deployment ensures logs for lifetime events and traceability as mandated.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Embedding logging is common compliance strategy. Acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Building instrumentation to comply with EU AI Act logging requirements is a standard, legally required business practice.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nProposal adds instrumentation hooks to existing experiment-tracking stack, emitting structured logs. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? It says ML-infrastructure team will add hooks. Might be some changes or reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Could be some changes. Implementability anchor: 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Embedding into pipeline is not trivial but feasible. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: Adding hooks to existing experiment tracking requires engineering effort and pipeline modifications but uses current infrastructure.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\nAnchors:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal bakes compliance into architecture, weaving logging into science. That sounds like paradigm stretching: changes relationship, logging woven into science. Not breaking? It doesn't change what lab researches. It says baking compliance into architecture avoids retrofits. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? Let's interpret: introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. However they say embed into training pipeline from day one. That is weaving logging into science. That's paradigm stretching definition: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science). So 3.\n\nEvidence: The proposal weaves logging into core R&D workflows rather than treating it as an after-the-fact compliance add-on.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nEmbedding logging into training pipeline is common practice? Many labs instrument pipelines. Might be common, mundane. Probably 1 or 2. The proposal is fairly standard compliance engineering. Likely common, mundane, boring => 1.\n\nEvidence: Integrating automated logging into ML pipelines is a common, widely discussed compliance engineering practice.\n\nReturn rating with id and subscale evidence and scores.\n\nFormat: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nLet's output structured.\n\n",
