blem of what lab researches, just how to comply. So 3.\n\nEvidence: proposal links compliance timing to R&D phase via a formal gate and SDK, weaving logging into scientific workflow rather than applying it uniformly.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nPhased logging with transition gate is probably interesting but not extremely rare? Might be unusual, interesting. Could be 3. Not ingenious surprising. I'd say 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nEvidence: two-tier logs and a science-to-system transition criterion with swappable instrumentation is an uncommon design for AI labs facing Article 12.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill phased logging meet mandate? The mandate requires high-risk AI systems technically allow automatic recording over lifetime. For prototypes not deployed, maybe okay? But Article 12 applies to high-risk AI systems. Early-stage prototypes may not be high-risk system yet? It says upgraded only at deployment-readiness gate. Might ensure no system reaches deployment without full traceability. So it will solve problem of meeting logging mandate while preserving iteration. Seems reasonable and will solve stated problem? Possibly partial because need to ensure logs for lifetime; but if prototypes never deployed, maybe fine. Effectiveness 4? \"will solve the stated problem without regard for workability\". It seems reasonable and would solve. I'd give 4.\n\nEvidence: by gating full Article 12(3) logging to deployment readiness, the lab can meet traceability requirements for any system released while keeping early research unencumbered.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = intervention not stated\n\nDirectly addresses how to meet logging mandate and adapt R&D. So 4.\n\nEvidence: the proposal directly targets the lab's decision of how to implement Article 12 logging without sacrificing exploratory iteration.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nBuilding shared SDK, defining transition criterion, two-tier methodology - some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Might be some changes. So 3.\n\nEvidence: creating a documented gate and reusable SDK requires engineering effort but is feasible within existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates laws\n\nPhased logging is acceptable, does not violate law if done correctly. Uncommon but not offensive. So 3? Could be 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Is phased compliance common? Might be somewhat uncommon. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: deferring full logging until deployment readiness is an unusual timing strategy but remains legally compliant and does not offend regulatory sensibilities.\n\nReturn JSON with id and evidence/score.\n\nNeed output format: echo its id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1457\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence each evidence.\n\nEnsure order: paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide integer level.\n\nProceed.",
