elationship. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Evidence: it adds a storage layer but keeps R&D and compliance separate.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious... Common, mundane? Tiered retrieval for logs is common. Might be 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting. The proposal says purpose-built storage layer with tiered retrieval - that's standard engineering. Probably common. So 1.\n\neffectiveness: degree will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? It addresses lifetime logging obligation technically unviable at scale. That seems core. Might be 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). The proposal directly enables automatic recording over lifetime with retrieval. So 4.\n\napplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes. So 4.\n\nProvide output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating... Might be lines. Provide structured.\n\nWe'll output:\n\nid: P2111\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence evidence for each.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Building a compliant long-horizon log store aligns with legal requirements and common engineering practice. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Creating a tiered columnar store requires new infrastructure work and load testing but uses established cloud storage patterns. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal adds logging tools while keeping compliance as a separate operational burden rather than redefining R&D. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Tiered hot-warm-cold log archiving with schema mapping to Article 12 is a standard, wid