te and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Mapping methodology addresses how to meet mandate. So 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem. Levels: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners). 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance.\n\nProposal turns compliance obligation into citable methodological output strengthening grant applications. That's changing relationship: compliance becomes research output. Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship. Could be 3. Not breaking? It doesn't change what lab researches fundamentally, just maps. Might be 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (pattern library) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Hmm \"Turns a compliance obligation into citable methodological output\" suggests changes relationship. I'd say 3.\n\noriginality: degree rare and ingenious. 4 not expressed before AND ingenious. 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 interesting. 1 common mundane boring.\n\nMapping methodology is somewhat unusual but maybe common in industry? Probably 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nProvide output with id and subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed: FIRST write one sentence of evidence for that subscale (your reasoning before the number). THEN give its score as integer.\n\nProbably output JSON or plain? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\".\n\nI'll produce structured lines.\n\nLet's craft one sentence evidence each.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Funding a six-month postdoc