        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence.\n\nWe need rating for P1054.\n\nProposal: Composable Logging Substrate at Scaffold Time. shared versioned logging library every new prototype imports day one... etc.\n\nRate each subscale.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nIs it common? Shared logging library, scaffold time. Might be unusual interesting shows some imagination. Not expressed before AND ingenious? Probably not that novel. It's a standard practice of shared libs and scaffolding. Might be interesting. Level 2 = interesting? Or 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. I'd say 2? Common compliance engineering overhead elimination via library is common. Maybe 2 = interesting. Could be 3? \"Composable Logging Substrate at Scaffold Time\" maybe unusual. Hmm.\n\nLet's think reference class: experienced evaluator expects AI R&D labs to give responses to mandate. Expected responses: just add logging, buy tool, hire compliance. A shared library wired into scaffolding is somewhat uncommon but not ingenious. Might be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nThis proposal introduces shared library and scaffolding, still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's level 2.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\nIdea does X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. Stated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal provides automatic logging substrate. So yes directly applies. Level 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill it solve? Guarantees every system technically satisfies automatic-logging mandate, eliminates per-project overhead. Seems reasonable and would solve main problem. Level 4? Might be partial? It addresses logging mandate. Yes 4.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\nTwo engineer-weeks to spec then wire into CLI. That's some changes reasonably feasible. Not trivial? Two engineer-weeks is low cost. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost. Might be 3. \"some changes\" fits. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Could argue 4. Hmm.\n\nSpec against Article 12(2)(a)-(c), wire library into scaffolding CLI so no prototype instantiated without logging. That's non-radical? Requires changing scaffolding. Might be easy. I'll give 3? The anchor: 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Two engineer-weeks is low cost. Maybe 4.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nShared logging library capturing timestamped events, model hashes, I/O payloads, risk-flag triggers. Capturing I/O payloads may raise privacy concerns? But compliance requires logs. It's common. Acceptable. Level 4.\n\nWe need evidence sentence for each.\n\nProvide output echo id exactly.\n\nFormat: probably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<