nce = paradigm stretching 3. Paradigm breaking requires changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Hmm \"places lab at knowledge frontier of compliance-by-design, generates citable methodology papers\" suggests changes relationship. Might be paradigm stretching not breaking? Breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship... It does introduce benchmark suite (new element) and changes relationship. Could be 4.\n\nLet's decide 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). This matches exactly. Paradigm breaking would be addresses larger problem of what lab researches? Might be borderline. I'll choose 3.\n\nOriginality: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious/imaginative/surprising. 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common.\n\nOpen-source benchmark for logging adequacy is unusual but maybe has been discussed. I'd say unusual, interesting; shows some imagination → 3.\n\nAcceptability: 4 = common strategies that violate no norms... 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. Publishing open-source benchmark aiding regulators is acceptable. Probably 4? It's common strategies? Uncommon but acceptable. Might be 3 somewhat uncommon. I'd say 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Actually releasing benchmarks is common academic practice. So 4.\n\nImplementability: 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Building benchmark suite with taxonomy, synthetic and real-system test cases, publish code — significant effort, requires research resources. Not easy low cost. Some changes reasonably feasible → 3. Could be 2 significant change expensive/difficult. Might be significant change. Hmm lab is R&D, building benchmark is core work, maybe moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nEffectiveness: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Does benchmark solve logging mandate? It helps define what suffices, but lab still must implement logging. It contributes to deciding how to meet mandate, not directly implement logs. So contributes partially → 3.\n\nApplicability: 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). The stated problem: decide how to respond, meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Benchmark helps interpret mandate but does not directly meet logging requirement for lab's own systems. It applies? It addresses implied problem of ambiguity in Article 12(2). That's related. Might be 3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y which applies). I'd say 3.\n\nProvide output format: echo id, then each subscale_evidence sentence then subscale score.\n\nNeed first write one sentence evidence for that subscale then give its score as integer level.\n\nProbably format:\n\nP1290\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n...\n\nLet's produce.\n\n",
