ty (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Writing schema validators in Python that run on PR. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Could be 3? Or 4 = easy low cost non-radical. Might require instrumentation of prototypes to emit logs for testing. That's some effort. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Not totally trivial.\n\nEvidence: It requires building schema validators and integrating them into existing CI/CD pipelines, which is feasible but needs engineering time and log instrumentation.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies violate no norms? Automated compliance gate is common. Score 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nEvidence: Blocking merges for non-conforming logs aligns with standard software quality practices and regulatory compliance expectations.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nCommon, mundane? CI/CD compliance gates are fairly common. Maybe interesting but not surprising. Originality 2 = interesting? Or 1 = common mundane. Automated log conformance gate is perhaps somewhat unusual for AI labs? Might be 2 = interesting. Let's pick 2.\n\nEvidence: Embedding Article 12 schema checks into CI/CD is a recognizable DevOps pattern applied to AI logging, not an ingenious surprise.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. Slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. The gate introduces new tools/processes but compliance remains logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching = 2? Definition: 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: st