tudies trade-offs. So maybe applicability 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nReasonable and will contribute to solution? It helps understand latency impact but does not ensure compliance logs are in place. It's partial solution. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Might be 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem? Actually it contributes to optimizing logging implementation. Probably effectiveness 3.\n\nNovelty originality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nQuantifying logging granularity vs latency is unusual? In AI systems research, trade-offs of instrumentation are known. Framing compliance as publishable research question maybe interesting but not ingenious. Likely originality 2 = interesting. Or 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Hmm. Might be 3? Let's pick 2? The instrument: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common. This is fairly standard systems evaluation. Could be interesting. I'll say originality 2.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal frames compliance instrumentation as publishable research question justifies engineering investment and positions lab at intersection... That's changes relationship? It weaves logging into science. Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). That's level 3. Slightly paradigm stretching would be introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Framing as publishable seems more than slightly. So paradigm_relatedness = 3.\n\nWorkability acceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Research on latency trade-offs is acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nSix-month sub-project with two PhD students run controlled ablation across three model families using new logging layer built under Proposal 1. Requires logging layer already built. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Might be some changes, or reasonably feasible measures = 3. Easy to implement at low cost? Needs 2 PhDs for 6 months + infrastructure. That's not trivial but feasible. Probably 3. Could be 2 = significant change, expensive/difficult. I'd say 3.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder listed: applicability, effectiveness, originality, paradigm_relatedness, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then score.\n\nLet's craft sentences:\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal studies how logging density impacts latency and throughput to inform implementation of Article 12 logging for high-risk models. Score 3? Actually description 3 = solves implied problem related. Could argue applicability 4? It says solves identified problem directly related... Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Y would be meeting mandate? It doesn't meet mandate, it studies trade-off. So not direct. Use 3.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Quantifying latency/memory trade-offs will contribute to choosing feasible logging settings but does not itself install compliant logs or ensure traceability. Score 3.\n\noriginality_evidence: Evaluating logging granularity versus inference performance is a recognizable systems instrumentation study rather than an ingenious or surprising reframing. Score 2? If we said interesting =2. Could be 3. Hmm.\n\nLet's decide originality 2 = interesting. Acceptable.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: By treating mandatory compliance instrumentation as a publishable systems research question, the proposal weaves logging into the lab's scientific agenda and changes the R&D-compliance relationship. Score 3.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Running controlled benchmarks on internal models to characterize logging overhead is socially, legally, and politically unobjectionable research practice. Score 4.\n\nimplementability_evidence: The work needs two PhD students for six months, existing logging layer, and access to three model families, which is a reasonably feasible but non-trivial commitment of scarce R&D resources. Score 3.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably just lines. Provide id P2419 then each.\n\nWill output structured.\n\n",
