bility: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Platform team builds Docker image with thin SDK; CI config declares dependency so no engineer writes custom log code. Keeps prototyping velocity flat. Sounds easy to implement at low cost, non-radical changes? Requires platform effort but reusable. Maybe some changes, reasonably feasible measures = 3. Easy to implement at low cost? Might need some changes. Could be 3. Let's say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms =4. Standardised logging sidecar is common. Acceptable. 4.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... Not expressed before AND ingenious? Sidecar pattern is common in microservices. For AI logging maybe unusual but not ingenious. Probably 2 = interesting? Or 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. Standardised logging sidecar for high-risk prototypes is fairly common practice. Might be common mundane boring =1. Hmm reference class: responses an experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Many would propose standardized logging library/sidecar. So common mundane. Originality 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem. Paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged =1. Slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice =2. The proposal keeps prototyping velocity flat because logging is inherited rather than rebuilt, gives one auditable schema. It introduces reusable containerised agent but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's definition of 2: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nCould also be 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance. I think sidecar is a bit more than standard; it's a tooling innovation. Choose 2.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably output lines.\n\nNeed for each subscale: \"<subscale>_evidence\" then \"<subscale>\". Order listed: applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nProvide id echo.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid P2471\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\n",
