ocially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Mandating compliance budget line is common? Somewhat uncommon? It's internal budgeting policy, not violating law. Likely 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Or 3 somewhat uncommon. Carving out 15% fixed non-negotiable is somewhat unusual but acceptable. Probably 4? Let's think: internal budget reallocation is common. Maybe 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. I'd say 3.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Requires updating templates and finance office enforce. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Updating template is relatively easy. Might be 3. Could be 4 easy? Enforcing at approval maybe requires change management. I'll give 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to problem. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving.\n\nBudget line makes resource tension explicit, changes relationship? It treats compliance as separate budget line, not weaving into science. Introduces new element (budget line) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? Maybe 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Yes.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious... Common mundane? Dedicated compliance budget line is fairly common in regulated industries. Might be interesting but not unusual. Could be 2 = interesting. Or 1 = common, mundane, boring. Fixed percentage allocation is a known project management practice. I'd say 2? Maybe 1 = common. Let's choose 2 = interesting (some imagination). Might be 1 common mundane. Hmm.\n\nThe reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Budget carve-out is plausible but not super original. Could be 2 interesting.\n\nLet's assign: applicability evidence sentence then score 4.\neffectiveness evidence then 3.\nacceptability evidence then 3? Or 4? Let's pick 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. It's internal finance policy, acceptable.\n\nImplementability 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness 2.\n\nOriginality 2? Might be 1. The anchored levels: 4 not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 unusual interesting shows imagination 2 interesting 1 common mundane boring. Fixed 15% line is fairly mundane. I'd go 1.\n\nLet's decide originality 2? Let's choose 2 for safety.\n\nProvide output format: echo id. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? The prompt says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Might be lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P1947\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nLet's do.\n\n",
